Q: Pointers in A Decomposition Function A: <p>Your function definition of <code>decompose</code> is correct. What is wrong is the way you are calling the function:</p>
<ol>
<li>A function call must be inside another function, such as <code>main</code>.</li>
<li>A function call must contain values, not types.</li>
</ol>
<p>If you makes these fixes, then your code will work. Here is an example:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

void decompose( double x, long *int_part, double *frac_part )
{
    *int_part = (long) x;
    *frac_part = x - *int_part;
}

int main(void)
{
    long int_part;
    double frac_part;

    decompose( 3.14, &amp;int_part, &amp;frac_part );

    printf( &quot;int part: %ld\nfrac part: %f\n&quot;, int_part, frac_part );
}
</code></pre>
<p>This program has the following output:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>int part: 3
frac part: 0.140000
</code></pre>
<p>Note however that as the user &quot;Andrew Henle&quot; has already pointed out, a <code>long</code> is not guaranteed to be able to represent the integer part of a <code>double</code>. To fix this, you can change the function <code>decompose</code> so that it checks whether the integer data type is large enough to store the integer part, and return a value that indicates an error if that is not the case. Here is an example:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;limits.h&gt;

bool decompose( double x, long *int_part, double *frac_part )
{
    if ( x &lt;= LONG_MIN || x &gt;= LONG_MAX )
    {
        return false;
    }

    *int_part = (long) x;
    *frac_part = x - *int_part;

    return true;
}

int main(void)
{
    long int_part;
    double frac_part;

    if ( decompose( 3.14, &amp;int_part, &amp;frac_part ) )
    {
        printf( &quot;int part: %ld\nfrac part: %f\n&quot;, int_part, frac_part );
    }
    else
    {
        printf( &quot;range error!\n&quot; );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note that due to floating-point imprecision, depending on the platform, it may be possible for the fractional part to be outside the range <code>-1.0</code> to <code>+1.0</code>.</p>
